{"reply":"A useful philosophical parallel is Socratic questioning. In Chapter 5, the Caterpillar begins with “Who are _you?_” and keeps pressing Alice to explain herself; when her answers fail to settle the issue, he simply returns to the same question. The calmness of the exchange makes her confusion feel less like a request for information than an examination she is expected to endure. ([Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/11/11-h/11-h.htm))

That resembles the Socrates described by the [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socrates/): a questioner who tries to make people think for themselves, often by confronting them with their own uncertainty. The important difference is that Socratic questioning is presented as potentially opening inquiry, while the Caterpillar’s purpose remains opaque—he may be testing Alice, provoking her, or simply refusing to make things easier. So the scene turns philosophical questioning into something unsettling: confusion may be intellectually productive, but it does not necessarily feel helpful while you are inside it.

